  <section style="background:var(--white);padding:2rem 0 3rem;">
    <div class="blog-post">
      <div class="blog-body">
        <h2>The Gas You Cannot See, Smell or Taste</h2>
        <p>Carbon monoxide (CO) is made when fuel does not burn fully. This happens when an appliance does not get enough air. CO has no colour, smell or taste. Once you breathe it in, it stops your blood carrying oxygen round your body. The first sign is often a headache. By the time worse symptoms start, you may be too confused to act.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://ukhsa.blog.gov.uk/2025/11/18/how-to-protect-your-home-and-family-from-the-invisible-danger-of-carbon-monoxide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UK Health Security Agency</a> says around 25 people die each year in England and Wales from accidental CO poisoning. Many more end up in hospital. Most carbon monoxide poisonings happen in autumn and winter, when heating is on. That is when we use our heating most and keep windows shut.</p>

        <div class="blog-stat">
          <div class="stat-num">25</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Around this many people die each year in England and Wales from accidental carbon monoxide poisoning, says the UK Health Security Agency.</div>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Got a plumbing problem now?" text="A real person answers 24/7. No call-out fee." />

        <h2>Why Liverpool Homes Face a Specific Risk</h2>
        <p>Liverpool has a lot of Victorian and Edwardian houses. Many still have old chimney flues, old gas fires or older boilers. A blocked or damaged flue is a common cause of CO building up in a home.</p>
        <p>Many homes have no CO alarm at all. Without one, you have no warning if an appliance starts to give off CO.</p>

        <h2>Which Appliances Produce Carbon Monoxide?</h2>
        <p>Anything that burns fuel can make CO if it is faulty, poorly kept or short of air. In homes, the main sources are:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Gas boilers</li>
          <li>Gas fires, including fuel-effect fires</li>
          <li>Gas cookers and hobs</li>
          <li>Blocked or damaged flues on any gas appliance</li>
          <li>Generators or camping stoves used indoors</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/domestic/co.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Health and Safety Executive</a> says around 7 people die each year from CO caused by gas appliances and flues that were not properly installed. Regular servicing and CO alarms help prevent this.</p>

        <h2>Symptoms of Carbon Monoxide Poisoning</h2>
        <p>CO poisoning is often mistaken for flu, food poisoning or just feeling tired. The key difference is that CO does not give you a high temperature. If your symptoms get better when you go out, and come back when you return, it could be CO. Act straight away.</p>

        <ul>
          <li>A headache that will not go, often in the morning</li>
          <li>Feeling dizzy or sick</li>
          <li>Being short of breath</li>
          <li>Tiredness and confusion</li>
          <li>Stomach pain</li>
          <li>Symptoms that ease when you leave the house and return when you go back</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="blog-warning">
          <strong>If you suspect carbon monoxide poisoning, act now</strong>
          <p>Get everyone out of the house now. Call 999 if anyone feels unwell. Call <a href="https://cadentgas.com/emergencies" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#b91c1c;">Cadent Gas on 0800 111 999</a> to report it. Do not go back in until a Gas Safe registered engineer has checked the house and said it is safe. See the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/carbon-monoxide-poisoning/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#b91c1c;">NHS carbon monoxide guidance</a> for medical advice.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Visual Warning Signs on Gas Appliances</h2>
        <p>You cannot see CO. But an appliance that is not burning properly often shows signs. Look out for these:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Yellow or orange flames, not a crisp blue one (fuel-effect fires are meant to look yellow)</li>
          <li>Soot, stains or black marks on or above an appliance</li>
          <li>More condensation than usual on windows in the room</li>
          <li>A pilot light that keeps going out</li>
          <li>A musty or odd smell near the boiler or gas fire</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to Protect Your Liverpool Home</h2>

        <h3>Install a carbon monoxide alarm</h3>
        <p>A CO alarm that sounds is the only reliable way to catch CO before you feel ill. Look for an alarm marked <a href="https://www.bsigroup.com/en-GB/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">British Standard BS EN 50291</a>. Fit one in each room with a gas appliance. Replace it when the maker says, often after 5 to 7 years.</p>
        <p>Since October 2022, landlords in England must fit a CO alarm in any room with a fixed appliance that burns fuel. Gas cookers do not count.</p>

        <h3>Book an annual gas safety inspection</h3>
        <p>A yearly check by a Gas Safe registered engineer is the best way to prevent CO. The engineer checks the flue, how well the appliance burns and all its safety devices. Landlords must have a valid <a href="/gas-safety-certificate-liverpool">CP12 gas safety certificate</a> every year. If you own your home, a yearly service is still wise. It also keeps most boiler warranties valid.</p>

        <h3>Never block ventilation</h3>
        <p>Gas appliances need air to burn safely. Never block air bricks, vents or flues. Never run a generator indoors. Never use a gas hob or barbecue to heat a room.</p>
        <CallStrip inline title="Want us to take a look?" text="Call for advice or a price. Gas Safe registered, no call-out fee." />

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">DETECTION</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How do you detect carbon monoxide if you cannot smell it?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>A CO alarm is the only reliable way. CO has no colour, smell or taste. Fit an alarm marked BS EN 50291 in each room with a gas appliance. Most alarms last 5 to 7 years.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">SYMPTOMS</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How do CO poisoning symptoms differ from flu?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>CO does not give you a high temperature. The main signs are headache, dizziness, feeling sick and tiredness. If you feel better when you go out and worse when you come back, it could be CO. Act straight away.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-navy">LANDLORDS</span><span class="pf-faq-q">Are landlords required to provide CO alarms in Liverpool?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Yes. Since October 2022, landlords in England must fit a CO alarm in any room with a fixed appliance that burns fuel. Gas cookers do not count. The council can fine you up to £5,000 if you do not comply.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-tag pf-tag-orange">PREVENTION</span><span class="pf-faq-q">How do I prevent carbon monoxide in my Liverpool home?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Fit CO alarms in each room with a gas appliance. Book a yearly check with a Gas Safe registered engineer. Never block vents. Look out for orange flames and soot marks. A yearly boiler service is the most important step.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

